    {
      "dom_id": "question-50133",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/9389d3d0d9206b72f379a328819e4624da3a294fc3222f048edc53957a8679ac.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/310d05d05284201c4914de171e6ee6a7574784da4c9bf7d4e442cdf2a210722d.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $x\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{1}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
