    {
      "dom_id": "question-80709",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/84597fa3c8e0a187df5792e8a8289033e6549599b1df5a34073049a5b3322770.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/3673d384035d7febd8540e34143c2a490f7aa3968752785ef809cff16d2524ce.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2}(2-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x<2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x.$",
