    {
      "dom_id": "question-86442",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/9000c061a8826c4492400671b3ee8e6700420e1e553ceda47e6f086a474211c2.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223512/assets/a71217d2b2e610b38f78f4471cc122de7098708900e3658cda449118df0e63dd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}.$",
